    "□ Mid-Evaluation Response (※ Key outputs: Self-assessment report, collection of supporting data, implementation plan for follow-up measures for interim evaluation) - Collection of supporting data for each self-evaluation index and preparation and submission of supporting data list - Organization and operation of the Self-Evaluation General Committee - Appropriateness check opinion → Self-assessment report reflected - Preparation and submission of implementation plan for follow-up measures to be pointed out and recommended by the upper inspection and general committee - Also, performance of all tasks in response to the interim evaluation from time to time  □ Comprehensive performance analysis (※ Key deliverables: Comprehensive performance analysis report, representation and introduction letter) - Collect performance low data for comprehensive performance analysis and analysis by performance type - Calculate performance and target achievement in strategic plan - Selection of representative excellence and submission of supporting data - Analysis of excellence and effectiveness of performance and suggestion of business improvement plan  □ Mid- to Long-term Strategic Planning (※ Key deliverables: Planning Report) - Organization and operation of mid- to long-term strategy establishment advisory committee - Derivation of mid- to long-term strategic direction and selection of key keywords through internal and external environmental analysis (PEST analysis, SWOT analysis) - Present a strategic plan (vision, strategic field, project) and detailed implementation plan - Presenting performance utilization plan and expected effect": 1,
    "The study was conducted to provide the sample design for 2024 Youth Risk Behavior Survey. The main purpose of the Youth Risk Behavior Survey is to produce the youth health behavior statistics. The 2023 year book of educational statistics is used for the sampling frame and its population is stratified into 39 strata of si/gun/gu to produce provincial regional level statistics and to improve highly precision of estimates. The size of sample is 400 schools in both of middle school and high school. The sample is allocated by modified proportional allocation into 17 provincial regions for each provincial region statistics having similar precision level and for reliable comparison results.  The allocated sample in each 17 provincial region is proportional allocated into stratum, then the allocated sample is proportional allocated into boy middle school, girl middle school and co-education middle school. High schools were stratified into general and specialized schools, and the proportional allocation method based on the number of classes was applied to boys' high schools, girls' high schools, and coeducational schools. The sample schools in each stratum are selected by CRN(Collocated Random Number) method in order to give an even distribution of the response burden among the schools in each stratum. The final sample weight for each sample student is the product of the design weight, the nonresponse adjustment and poststratification adjustment factors. The design weight is calculated as the reciprocal of the sample student’s probability of selection. The nonresponse adjustment is calculated by the inverse of response rate and the post-stratification adjustment factor is computed by ratio of the number of boy/girl students and sum of nonresponse adjusted weight. In order to improve the sample design for the 2025 survey, we proposed a study on ways to quickly respond to changes in population characteristics that may occur in the future through continuous monitoring of the population analysis results. In addition, research on survey quality monitoring and evaluation methods through systematic management and analysis of paradata was proposed. ": 1,
    "○ Objective - The objective of this study is to analyze the indicators affected by the change in survey methods to identify the influencing factors of these changes. Additionally, the study seeks to develop improvements to the survey questionnaire through cognitive interviews.  ○ Content - Comparative Analysis of Indicator Changes Resulting from Survey Method Changes : Yearly (2021 vs. 2022) and age group (under 60 vs. over 60) comparison of indicators affected by the change in survey methods, and comparative analysis of the 2024 National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) test-retest data. - Cognitive Interviews and Improvement of Survey Questions  ○ Results - Five indicators, including [Activities limitation], [Father’s education level], [Mother’s education level], [EQ-5D Index], and [Weekday sleep time], showed similar trends in both 2021 and 2022. These trends were observed in both the self-administered group (under 60) and the interview group (over 60), with both groups exhibiting either an increase or a decrease in the indicators. Results from logistic regression analyses, adjusted for gender, age, income, education level, occupation, and residential area (urban/rural), also indicated similar trends. As an indicator showing similar trends in two age groups with different survey methods, these results may reflect both the differences due to changes in the survey methods and the social characteristics of 2022. - The indicators that showed differing trends between the results of 2021 and 2022 included [Monthly sick bed experience], [Unmet need health care utilization)], [Outpatient utilization for 2 weeks], [Influenza vaccination], and [Weekend sleep time]. These indicators mostly exhibited increasing or decreasing trends in the self-administered group under 60. Results from logistic regression analyses, adjusted for gender, age, income, education level, occupation, and residential area (urban/rural), also indicated similar trends. A similar trend was observed in the [Health screening], which was assessed through interviews across all age groups. - Based on the results of the first and second rounds of cognitive interviews, improvements to the survey questionnaire were developed. The proposed enhancements included adjusting the order of questions, restructuring response categories, revising questions for easier understanding, and adding explanatory notes. Subsequently, feedback from the relevant department (KCDC) was incorporated to propose a final revised version. - The final revised version, incorporating feedback from the relevant department (KCDC), aimed to maintain the original questionnaire as much as possible while considering the consistency of the questions, the convenience for survey administrators, and the understanding of respondents. Suggestions were made for the online questionnaire design, including highlighting (such as font color and size) and pop-up displays for reference periods; however, it was confirmed that these features could not be implemented in the current survey system. Therefore, the original version was retained, with improvements made by indicating reference periods for some questions and adding explanatory notes to the response options.": 1,
    "◦ The 10th KNHANES used the enumeration district(ED) of 2022 Census as sampling frame. We divided up into non-apartment resident and apartment resident, and we designed to do the rolling sample survey. Summarizing the major population change characteristics, the proportion of urban dwellings has increased, the proportion of families in 1 and 2 person households has increased, and the proportion of households living in apartments has increased. ◦ The 10th sample design maintains the framework of the current sample design and reflects the changed population characteristics. We stratified into 17 provisional region of non-apartment resident and apartment resident, and again stratified as cluster analysis using CCC within each 1st stratum. The 3rd stratum is Dong-Eup/Myun within 2nd stratum. ◦ The number of sample units(PSUs) for the 10th KNHANES is 576, the number of sample households(SSUs) is about 12,000. PSU is the ED, SSU is household. We used the combined ED which added one selected ED and the neighbor ED. The resident size of the combined ED(or the wide-area ED) is about 150 HHs. We proposed completing an average of 25 households from the ED, taking into account the results of analysis of sample data and the efficiency of survey management. ◦ Analysis of the KNHANES data requires the use of weights based on non-response adjustments and the auxiliary information of population. We considered the statistical validity of the weights currently using the 8th and 9th data, and proposed possible improvements. We have verified that the current weights satisfy the properties of the underlying estimates and the weighting process is statistically valid. ◦ We proposed that an artificial process of devaluation should not be performed by preventing the occurrence of extreme weights in advance. This requires easing of constraints that apply to existing post-stratification methods. This method is almost identical or superior in statistical properties and statistical efficiency compared to existing weights.": 1,
    "The COVID-19 virus was first detected in December 2019, and in March 2020, the WHO declared a pandemic, the highest risk level among infectious disease warnings, and the WHO and CDC officially recognized the possibility of airborne infection of the COVID-19 virus in an enclosed environment in May 2021.  This study conducted a pilot project to evaluate the risk of air transmission in 17 cities and provinces using the already developed K-VENT (K-VENT) for the risk of air transmission in 17 cities and provinces. In addition, it was conducted with the aim of expanding education for local governments and infectious disease management support groups and enhancing practical utilization.  Meanwhile, the previously developed K-VENT program is characterized by being able to easily calculate natural and mechanical ventilation and calculate the risk of air infection for respiratory infectious diseases. In this study, additional functions were developed through pilot projects, and the main results are summarized as follows.  (1) A pilot evaluation was carried out in 16 cities and provinces on infection-vulnerable facilities, evaluating the risk of air transmission for 33 facilities and training on evaluation methods was completed for 129 people in charge of infectious diseases. (2) The function of calculating the carbon dioxide concentration of the space to be evaluated was added, and the DB storage and expression function were supplemented to improve the convenience of use. (3) Educational materials were developed to strengthen the practical capabilities of air propagation risk assessment, and practical training was conducted twice for ventilation evaluation personnel. (4) Guidelines for facilities vulnerable to infection were established based on the implications derived through the use of air transmission risk assessment tools and pilot projects.": 1,
    "To establish the capacity of personnel who can perform basic infection control work in infection-vulnerable facilities and to establish a plan to introduce a qualification system to secure the infrastructure for normal infection control and response to infectious disease outbreaks in infection-vulnerable facilities  The core infection control work capacity based on the National Competency Standards (NCS) is as follows: establishment of infection prevention and control plan, infection prevention and control education and training, cooperation with related organizations, self-inspection of facilities, blocking of pathogen inflow, compliance with quarantine rules in facilities, facility environment management, support for epidemiological investigation in case of infectious disease outbreak, initial response and cohort isolation measures in case of infectious disease crisis  The results of the FGI implementation on the introduction of a national qualification system for infection control personnel are as follows: The infection control agent system is defined as a system that appoints an infection prevention manager to be in charge of infection prevention management work in social welfare facilities of a certain size or larger where vulnerable groups such as the elderly, the disabled, and the mentally ill reside, and proposes introducing it as a national professional qualification accredited by the Korea Disease Control and Prevention Agency by establishing a legal basis. In order to lower the barrier to introducing the qualification, the level is divided into two levels and career recognition and concurrent job recognition are allowed so that qualifications can be acquired. In addition, it is proposed to make the infection control assistant system mandatory in order to further strengthen the infection control capacity of facilities of a certain size or larger.  The introduction of the system should be approached in stages with a long-term perspective. It is expected that the purpose will be achieved if the system is introduced while supplementing it.  If a policy alternative is prepared due to realistic constraints, the dispatch of external experts is proposed. This will be a plan that can reduce the workload of internal personnel and increase the infection control effectiveness of the facility with professional knowledge and leadership.": 1,
    "● Development and Pilot Implementation of Community-Based Intervention Protocols for Particulate Matter Management with Universality and Differentiation  ● Intervention Model and Protocol for Older Adults - Utilizing home-visit healthcare services and senior welfare centers (e.g., senior citizens' community centers), the project integrates educational and behavioral interventions delivered by local service providers (e.g., home healthcare teams) or community leaders (e.g., \"Smart Companion\" teams). These interventions aim to mitigate health risks associated with particulate matter exposure. - To evaluate feasibility and practicality, pilot implementations were conducted in: Dong-gu, Incheon (centered on home-visit healthcare services), Yangcheon-gu, Seoul (centered on senior welfare centers), and Chuncheon, Gangwon-do (a mixed model). - Feedback was collected through interdisciplinary intervention advisory committees, expert workshops, and community stakeholder consultations in Incheon, Seoul, and Gangwon regions. Insights from pilot operations were incorporated into the model and protocol development.  ● Intervention Model and Protocol for Children - Targeting elementary schools in regions with high particulate matter exposure, the project implemented educational and behavioral interventions through local service providers (e.g., homeroom teachers, environmental educators). These interventions include cognitive-behavioral education, participatory behavioral education, and classroom-based particulate matter control measures. - Pilot implementations were conducted to verify feasibility: cognitive-behavioral education was piloted in Chungcheongnam-do, and participatory behavioral interventions were piloted in Ulsan.  ● Intervention Model and Protocol for Occupationally Exposed Populations - For outdoor workers, the educational and behavioral intervention models leveraging workplace resources, such as occupational health managers, to mitigate health risks associated with particulate matter exposure. - To evaluate feasibility and practicality, pilot implementations were conducted at two workplaces with outdoor workers in Dong-gu, Incheon.  ● Intervention Model and Protocol for Wildfire-Related Particulate Matter Exposure - A manual was developed for mitigating the health impacts of particulate matter exposure during and after wildfire events, with a focus on the Gangwon region.": 1,
    "○Establishment of research base and follow-up of COPD patients infected with COVID-19 ○ Analysis of clinical course and prognosis of COPD patients after COVID-19 infection, detection of biomarker 1.Using the pre-established registry, check the clinical features and sequale of COVID-19 infection in COPD patients 2. Collect the chest CT images and pulmonary function tests of COPD patients infected with COVID-19, and analyze chest image readings using an automatic quantification analysis technique 3. For the sequale such as pulmonary fibrosis or pulmonary embolism, link and analyze the claim data 4. By checking baseline and follow-up information, identify Korean-specific biomarkers with associated with pulmonary fibrosis, pulmonary embolism and acute exacerbation after COVID-19 infection": 1,
    "ㅇ In order to improve the utilization of pathogen resources, the overall system and details of the law are reviewed, and amendments are proposed in the following areas: (1) resource management, (2) specialized banks, (3) depository and other areas. To this end, the legislative practices of overseas genetic resource donor and recipient countries were reviewed, and even those that do not currently require amendment were reviewed for their effectiveness and necessity, to provide a basis for the appropriateness of maintaining the current provisions.  ㅇ (1) In terms of resource management, changes are proposed to the chairperson and composition of the Pathogen Resource Review Committee to emphasize the importance of the committee and secure specialized seats. (2) Proposed to revise the timing of the survey so that the results of the survey can be incorporated into the comprehensive plan. The selection of targets for overseas export approval should be left to the discretion of the Director of KCDC, and only basic principles should be set to allow for flexibility, but non-commercial export should be exempted from approval to increase academic utilization.  ㅇ (2) In the case of specialized banks, a separate set of facilities and equipment standards is set out alongside the existing LMO Tier 2 facilities standards to broaden the designation standards. In addition, the revision sets out a procedure to be followed if a designated specialized bank wishes to withdraw its designation. To ensure that Specialty Banks are financially compensated for the sale of resources, the existing \"fee\" provision is changed to \"sale fee\" to clarify the authority of Specialty Banks to collect fees, while maintaining the current National Pathogen Resource Bank fee provision that requires Specialty Banks to collect the same fee for listed resources.  ㅇ (3) Revision changed the deposit period to encourage the utilization and reflection of resources. At the same time, the revision allows deposit performance to be considered in the evaluation of national R&D projects and subsequent project applications so that to support active deposit. Proposed changes to broaden the definition of \"infectious disease\", a key concept, to include unknown infectious diseases that may arise in the future.": 1,
    "ㅇ Background: There is a growing need for establishing AI-enabled disease surveillance system against the spread of infectious diseases both domestically and globally.  ㅇ Objective: This pilot research project aimed to establish an AI surveillance framework and evaluate its validity/applicability to be considered for the long-term development of a national smart disease surveillance system in Korea. ㅇ Method: A literature review was performed for the current status of domestic and international disease surveillance systems. Comparative analyses were performed on surveillance targets, participating institutions, data utilized, prediction methods, and case studies. The project involved the development of an AI-based surveillance framework, including the construction of AI surveillance models, assessment of prediction accuracy, and the automation of data collection, and data integration and preprocessing in a cloud environment. The framework was used to evaluate the usability and validity of the disease surveillance system. ㅇ Results: This project derived an AI modeling framework to predict the time series trends and risk levels of infectious diseases in key regions across Korea. To enhance the prediction accuracy, optimization strategies were proposed, including data integration, cloud-based collection, data preprocessing, variable selection, model selection, and parameter tuning. The optimization strategies significantly improved the prediction accuracy, reliability, and stability of the AI surveillance modeling. Additionally, advanced international cases and visualization ways of AI surveillance results were discussed for their potential introduction in domestic settings. ㅇ Conclusion: This project suggested the high potential (prediction accuracy and stability) of utilizing AI models, big data, and cloud systems for disease surveillance (i.e., COVID-19 exemplified in this project). The results suggested the future establishment and independent operation of the AI disease surveillance system that can complement the functions of the existing medical surveillance practices. The results of this project can represent important reference data for national infectious disease management, response, and policy formulation regarding many infectious diseases. Additionally, this project suggested the need for establishing and expanding AI-based surveillance systems for not only COVID-19 but also other nationally designated and emerging infectious diseases in Korea.": 1
